WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.980
Film Courage: Peter when we first met you
(I believe it was 2014 in the summer time),

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:11.860
so it’s almost been four years now exactly
and we heard from you (in this interview)

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.640
and it’s done incredibly well (as all of
your’s have) but this I’d love to play

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.770
it for you if you don’t mind?

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.160
Peter Russell, story doctor, instructor and
screenwriter: Sure.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.160
Film Courage: Great.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.880
I know this sort of feels like a Senate Hearing
but it’s not.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.510
Peter: I didn’t do anything criminal.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:24.640
Film Courage: Good.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.640
Peter: I remember that.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.520
I’ve got my lawyer in the back.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.520
Film Courage: Good.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.481
No this is about…we will keep in on screenwriting
here.

00:00:30.481 --> 00:00:32.770
And we have a great clip to play, so here
we go.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:33.770
[Audio]

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.632
[Peter Russell from our first interview in
2014 at Story Expo entitled I Don’t Enjoy

00:00:35.632 --> 00:00:36.632
Writing Screenplays]

00:00:36.632 --> 00:00:38.020
“That I’m no good at it.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:43.630
The reason that I’m a teacher and a consultant
and a story doctor is that my big secret is

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.030
that I don’t enjoy writing screenplays.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.890
I tried for awhile to do it, it’s not my
thing.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.682
If poetry paid, I’d be in the chips because
I’m a poet because that is what I love to

00:00:54.682 --> 00:00:56.500
write and I also like to write novels.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.430
But years ago the reason I became what I am
is because years ago I failed to complete

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.430
a screenplay.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.940
It got huge interest and I realized at the
end of it was I don’t like this form, it’s

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:08.940
just not for me.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.590
I love analyzing it, I love understanding
how it works, I love helping other people

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:21.640
do it but I don’t enjoy sitting down and
writing a screenplay form, it’s not my thing.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.480
Film Courage: Okay, great.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.900
So that was four years ago.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:32.340
And what I’ve found from an email from you
is that you’ve actually completed an historical

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.340
TV series.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.170
Peter: Yeah and I still hate writing.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.950
Film Courage: Okay, so nothing has change.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.880
Peter: I’m always going to hate it.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.830
I was just telling Suzy yesterday “Gosh,
I hate this!”

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:48.700
But I’ll tell you yes, I’ve had some success
and what’s great about that is…here’s

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:55.300
the really great thing, when you’re a teacher
you don’t really know for sure…my stuff

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.890
actually helps, actually turns out to work.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:03.720
The stuff I’ve been teaching actually has
helped me which is a huge relief because when

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.369
you’re a teacher you are often up there
and it’s easy to pontificate “Oh do this

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:11.170
and try this tool.”

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.560
Some of my tools were terrible, but a lot
of them actually worked.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.959
But I still hate writing and that’s always
probably going to be the case and I think

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:25.140
maybe there are some writers who don’t hate
writing, they just love it.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.550
“Oh, I can’t wait to get to the desk.”

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.880
But I’m always going to loathe it.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.200
I guess I’m good enough at it to get paid
and so I’ll do it.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.450
It’s actually fun to get paid.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:48.840
And it’s fun to know that your stuff is
actually going to be out there but yeah I’ll

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:58.300
tell you something else I’ve been consulting
and ghost writing and story doctoring and

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.480
teaching for 20 years.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.099
And I quit writing, I quit the whole idea
that I was going to write at least 6 times

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.770
“Screw this!”

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.770
And I meant it.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.670
“This is terrible, it’s awful.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.590
I’m never going to succeed and I stopped.”

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.980
And I stopped, I stopped for at least a couple
of years.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:24.160
And I did that several times because the process
of writing is so excruciating I think for

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.840
me and other people.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:34.849
I have to say my lovely bride Suzy is a very
successful writer in South Africa.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:43.400
I hope she doesn’t mind me telling this
story but she is very successful in South

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:44.790
Africa.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:48.360
She has her own column and she’s created
her own voice.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:55.269
She came here and she started studying television
(she’s frowning at me, I probably shouldn’t

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:56.269
say this).

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.660
Anyway, my Gosh this is hard!

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:00.840
And it is hard.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:07.350
The film and television structure is difficult,
it’s a difficult form to master and it has

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.071
so many different components to master.

00:04:09.071 --> 00:04:14.010
I think if you’re a writer (if you are a
novelist), it’s difficult.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:22.620
All writing is hard, but with television and
film it’s excruciatingly complex, there

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:31.879
are so many moving parts to that it may be
one of the harder writing forms.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:38.520
And it was certainly the case for me and that’s
the case so yeah I stand by the fact that

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.520
I hate writing.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.949
Film Courage: So what changed (sorry to interrupt),
what changed for you from the time we shot

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:44.949
that interview?

00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:51.379
Peter: I guess I got people who wanted to
pay me to co-write with them as opposed to

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:56.931
story doctor and so because of that…and
I also found the right partner…here this

00:04:56.931 --> 00:05:01.639
is critical especially when you are writing
in television and film.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:06.939
I know people who do it alone and they are
great, I don’t know how that’s even possible.

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:13.110
I highly recommend that you get a partner
(a writing partner).

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.569
I’ve had a couple in the last year that
have been marvelous.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:24.770
And when you find the right partner it’s
almost like you’ve gone from the single’s

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.919
bar to a couple, it’s like “Oh, yeah!

00:05:28.919 --> 00:05:29.919
I love you.

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:34.210
I want you here and you can help and I’m
not alone.”

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.909
And that’s really what it feels like.

00:05:35.909 --> 00:05:38.990
And also they admire you and they tell you
you’re great.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:44.050
And you need someone telling you you’re
great, when you’re a writer because you

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.710
feel like crap all the time.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:52.169
And you are also getting producers and managers
and agents whose main job is to tell you you’re

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:53.360
crap, that’s what they do.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.169
“This is crap!”

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:58.300
This is pretty much…just put that on auto-play.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.449
“Oh, my manager is calling.

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:01.449
What are they going to say?

00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:02.969
They are going to say this is crap.”

00:06:02.969 --> 00:06:04.599
And that’s what they do “This is crap!

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:05.659
How dare you.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:08.059
I just talked to someone about this story.

00:06:08.059 --> 00:06:09.219
Well they say this is crap, Peter.

00:06:09.219 --> 00:06:10.439
It’s absolute crap.”

00:06:10.439 --> 00:06:16.199
And I have to say again, that is also what
you’ve got to get used to in the commercial

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:21.840
field which is also I think William Goldman
said this a long time ago and I didn’t realize

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.419
it, he said everything is a piece of sh*t.

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:28.199
When they refer to your script, they may have
bought it for a million dollars he said “Hey,

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:29.680
I just read your piece of sh*t.”

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.589
Or “We are filming your piece of sh*t next
week.”

00:06:32.589 --> 00:06:34.729
And it’s a piece of sh*t, right.

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:40.879
So anybody who feels like “Well I need to
be validated and I need to be affirmed”

00:06:40.879 --> 00:06:48.059
he should not go into Hollywood writing business
because whatever the culture that originated

00:06:48.059 --> 00:06:51.319
in Hollywood and I think it was and I think
it was a bad deal for writers back in the

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:56.990
30’s (we gave up our birthright for sod
pottage), where we said “Hey, we don’t

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.120
need our name on it.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.539
It’s not our movie.”

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:05.800
Whatever happened back then there’s a toxic
relationship that was commemorated back then

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.180
by the writer and the producer.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:13.129
And the relationship was the producer is in
charge and the writer is sort of servile and

00:07:13.129 --> 00:07:17.789
they’re going to get the abuse and whenever
there is a problem, it’s usually a problem

00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:18.789
for the writer.

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:22.241
It’s not the producer’s problem, it’s
the writer’s problem, he just didn’t write

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.090
it well enough.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:31.270
So you are going to get a lot of criticism
and abuse even when they say they like your

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:37.589
thing, even when they buy your thing, it’s
still going to be “It’s a piece of sh*t.”

00:07:37.589 --> 00:07:41.449
And that just sort of what it’s been like
in my experience.

00:07:41.449 --> 00:07:44.990
So you need to writing partner to grab your
hand “That’s okay darling, yes we are

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:45.990
great aren’t we?

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:47.389
Yes we are, it doesn’t matter what they
say.

00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:49.099
Yes, we’re great.”

00:07:49.099 --> 00:07:51.020
And that’s what writing partners can do
for you.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:52.580
I highly-recommend, do not do this alone.

